## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical knowledge of testing streaming systems, with emphasis on edge-case window behavior, fault tolerance, and end-to-end accuracy—skills essential for building production-grade data platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Windowing semantics and latency boundaries
- Idempotent and deduplicated alerting
- End-to-end streaming integration patterns
- Backpressure handling and burst tolerance
- Observability and feature drift detection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Unit test for window aggregation correctness
async function testWindowAggregation() {
  const windowService = new WindowingService({
    windowSizeMs: 5000,
    slideIntervalMs: 1000
  });

  // Simulate late-arriving events within watermark tolerance
  await windowService.processEvents([
    { timestamp: 1000, value: 10 },
    { timestamp: 2000, value: 20 },
    { timestamp: 5500, value: 30 } // Late event within tolerance
  ]);

  const result = await windowService.getWindowResult();
  expect(result.sum).toBe(60);
  expect(result.count).toBe(3);
}

// Integration test for alert deduplication
async function testAlertDeduplication() {
  const alertService = new AlertService();

  // Simulate duplicate events from Kafka retry
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    await alertService.process({
      eventId: 'evt-123',
      anomalyScore: 0.95,
      timestamp: Date.now()
    });
  }

  const alerts = await alertService.getEmittedAlerts();
  expect(alerts.length).toBe(1); // Only one alert emitted
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to validate a production-grade streaming ML pipeline under realistic conditions, covering data integrity, performance under load, drift handling, and safe deployment strategies.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming test strategies and synthetic data generation
- Backpressure handling and consumer lag in Kafka/Flink
- Feature drift detection and model retraining triggers
- Canary rollouts, shadow traffic, and safe rollback procedures

## Code Example
```javascript
// End-to-end test harness wiring synthetic replay, latency tracking,
// and assertion validation
async function runE2ETest(config) {
  const syntheticData = generateLabeledAnomalies(config);
  const results = await pipeline.process(syntheticData);

  const latency = measureLatency(results);
  assert(latency.p95 < 300, 'P95 latency exceeded budget');
  assert(latency.p99 < 1000, 'P99 latency exceeded budget');

  const correctness = validateChecksums(results);
  assert(correctness, 'Data correctness check failed');

  return { latency, correctness };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamental TDD understanding and ability to follow red-green-refactor cycle - essential for writing maintainable code at Anthropic.

## Expected Answer
Candidate should explain: 1) Write failing test (red), 2) Write minimal code to pass (green), 3) Improve code while tests pass (refactor). Should demonstrate writing test first, then implementation.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Red: Write failing test
test('calculator adds numbers', () => {
  const calc = new Calculator();
  expect(calc.add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
});

// Green: Minimal implementation
class Calculator {
  add(a: number, b: number): number {
    return a + b;
  }
}

// Refactor: Extract if needed
```

